WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.020
So what if I told you that the reason your favorite app crashed this morning is basically the exact same reason that two cooks can completely ruin a cake?

00:09.440 --> 00:09.560
Right.

00:09.660 --> 00:17.180
Or like why a library can lose a priceless book or even why a surgeon has to kick everyone out of an operating room.

00:17.280 --> 00:18.280
It's a bold claim.

00:18.540 --> 00:22.300
It is. But today we're diving into the physics of logic.

00:22.720 --> 00:27.540
Yeah. And, you know, it sounds like a massive stretch when you put it like that, but it really isn't.

00:27.600 --> 00:27.840
Right.

00:27.840 --> 00:34.600
We are so used to thinking of the physical world and the digital world as operating on like completely different sense of rules.

00:34.940 --> 00:35.840
Totally different reality.

00:36.040 --> 00:36.400
Exactly.

00:36.600 --> 00:36.740
Yeah.

00:36.880 --> 00:45.680
But the underlying mechanics of how things actually change and crucially, who is allowed to change them, they're entirely universal.

00:46.060 --> 00:53.680
You know, usually when we talk about diagnosing a problem in the real world, like a medical diagnosis, there is this baseline expectation of precision.

00:53.980 --> 00:54.260
Oh, sure.

00:54.340 --> 00:55.440
It's like engineering, right?

00:55.440 --> 00:58.120
Right. You fall off your bike, your arm hurts, you go to the hospital.

00:58.280 --> 00:59.140
Yeah. And you get an x-ray.

00:59.720 --> 01:08.760
Exactly. The x-ray shows that jagged white line on the dark film and the doctor just points right to it and says, you know, there it is. That's the break.

01:09.220 --> 01:10.400
Right. It's binary.

01:10.680 --> 01:10.880
Yeah.

01:10.920 --> 01:16.220
And more importantly, it's undeniable. I mean, the bone is either broken or it's not broken.

01:16.300 --> 01:17.620
Right. You can't really argue with it.

01:17.620 --> 01:22.580
No, it's clean. You can see the absolute reality of the situation right there in black and white.

01:22.640 --> 01:26.680
And it's comforting, honestly. Like we desperately want things to be visible.

01:26.840 --> 01:27.320
Oh, absolutely.

01:27.520 --> 01:32.280
We want things to be categorized neatly without any ambiguity at all.

01:32.280 --> 01:42.760
We want to know that the rules of the physical universe, you know, like bone density and the force of gravity that are actively enforcing what we are looking at.

01:42.820 --> 01:44.940
Right. A bone can't just pretend to be unbroken.

01:45.080 --> 01:48.640
Exactly. The physical universe just does not allow for logical contradiction.

01:48.640 --> 01:52.520
It doesn't. But then then you step into the world of complex systems.

01:52.780 --> 01:53.180
Oh, boy.

01:53.460 --> 02:07.140
And I mean, anything really from a sprawling legal contract with like 100 sub clauses to a city's plumbing infrastructure to, frankly, the software that runs our entire modern existence.

02:07.420 --> 02:07.520
Right.

02:07.700 --> 02:12.740
Suddenly, that beautiful, precise x-ray machine is just broken.

02:13.100 --> 02:14.480
You can't see the break anymore.

02:14.480 --> 02:20.160
No, you are looking at a diagnostic landscape that is, well, honestly, it's completely murky.

02:20.380 --> 02:26.740
You're trying to figure out why a system crashed or why a legal loophole was exploited.

02:27.000 --> 02:29.580
And you realize the rules weren't physical laws.

02:29.820 --> 02:30.520
What were they?

02:30.600 --> 02:31.660
They were just suggestions.

02:31.780 --> 02:33.560
Just suggestions. Wow.

02:33.760 --> 02:37.560
It is the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

02:37.860 --> 02:38.200
Yeah.

02:38.200 --> 02:44.840
When the rules are just conventions that people politely agree to follow, like a sign on a door saying, please keep closed.

02:44.960 --> 02:45.200
Right.

02:45.420 --> 02:51.620
Rather than physical laws that they are actually forced to obey, like, you know, a solid brick wall, you don't have an x-ray anymore.

02:51.760 --> 02:52.300
No, you don't.

02:52.620 --> 02:58.020
You just have a very long, very unreliable transcript of rumors about what might have happened.

02:58.020 --> 03:02.620
And let me tell you, a transcript of rumors is a complete nightmare to debug.

03:02.720 --> 03:03.660
I can only imagine.

03:03.820 --> 03:10.920
Because when a system allows people to just, you know, bypass the rules quietly, you lose the ability to trust anything you're actually looking at.

03:11.160 --> 03:13.560
Which is exactly why we are here today.

03:13.760 --> 03:16.900
So welcome to a brand new deep dive.

03:17.080 --> 03:18.300
So glad to be here for this one.

03:18.300 --> 03:26.520
Yeah, we designed this one specifically for you, the listener, the lifelong learner who loves those, like, visceral aha moments.

03:26.660 --> 03:26.900
Yes.

03:27.100 --> 03:33.520
The person who wants the profound insights but wants to skip the overwhelming, super dense jargon.

03:33.620 --> 03:35.340
Which is very easy to get bogged down in.

03:35.400 --> 03:35.980
Oh, completely.

03:36.420 --> 03:40.460
So today we are exploring a truly fascinating manuscript.

03:40.940 --> 03:46.580
It's actually an essay by an independent researcher named Flyxion, and it's called Thinking Like Rust.

03:46.580 --> 03:46.700
Rust.

03:47.280 --> 03:50.920
And we really should set the mission right out of the gate here.

03:51.000 --> 03:51.460
Good idea.

03:51.580 --> 03:57.240
Because I know for a fact that some of you just heard the word rust and immediately thought, oh no.

03:57.400 --> 03:57.620
Right.

03:57.740 --> 03:59.100
I clicked the wrong deep dive.

03:59.320 --> 03:59.500
Yeah.

03:59.680 --> 04:01.580
I accidentally queued up a programming tutorial.

04:01.740 --> 04:02.540
I don't know how to code.

04:02.660 --> 04:03.040
I'm out.

04:03.260 --> 04:04.000
Please don't leave.

04:04.120 --> 04:05.040
Wait, let me stop you right there.

04:05.340 --> 04:07.420
This is absolutely not a syntax guide.

04:07.520 --> 04:07.700
No.

04:07.820 --> 04:11.980
This isn't about, you know, semicolons or how to compile software.

04:12.420 --> 04:13.420
No, not at all.

04:13.420 --> 04:21.680
What we're doing today is looking at the fundamental, completely inevitable laws of logic that govern systems.

04:22.100 --> 04:22.520
Any system.

04:22.660 --> 04:23.420
Any system.

04:23.740 --> 04:39.180
The author of this manuscript makes this incredibly compelling argument, which is that the notoriously strict rules of the Rust programming language aren't just like arbitrarily hoops that a pedantic compiler makes a programmer jump through.

04:39.180 --> 04:43.920
They are actually rigorous formalizations of how the physical world actually works.

04:44.020 --> 04:44.900
It's all about physics.

04:45.020 --> 04:45.180
Yeah.

04:45.420 --> 04:52.840
We're talking about the underlying logic of baking a cake, checking out a library book, or, as we'll get into later, performing open heart surgery.

04:52.940 --> 04:54.860
It's really a structural exploration of why.

04:54.920 --> 04:55.100
Okay.

04:55.200 --> 04:55.900
Lay it out for us.

04:56.040 --> 05:00.100
Well, the author introduces this concept, and it's called admissibility.

05:00.580 --> 05:01.060
Admissibility.

05:01.060 --> 05:01.340
Yeah.

05:01.660 --> 05:08.220
Basically, admissibility is the exact set of conditions under which a transition from one state to another is actually legitimate.

05:08.440 --> 05:08.740
Okay.

05:08.800 --> 05:18.160
It's about looking at how we can build systems that don't just politely ask us to be careful, but structurally, physically prevent us from making mistakes.

05:18.520 --> 05:24.120
Systems that replace the please keep door closed sign with that solid brick wall we talked about.

05:24.120 --> 05:24.640
Exactly.

05:24.900 --> 05:25.060
Okay.

05:25.100 --> 05:35.760
Let's unpack this, because I was honestly jumping out of my chair reading how this document uses, like, everyday phenomena to explain these dense, super abstract concepts.

05:35.900 --> 05:36.700
It's really brilliant.

05:36.800 --> 05:37.200
It is.

05:37.900 --> 05:46.780
So, before we can even begin to understand the rules of a system, the author says we first have to define what the system actually is.

05:46.940 --> 05:49.240
We have to fundamentally shift our perspective.

05:49.440 --> 05:49.660
Right.

05:49.840 --> 05:53.540
We have to stop looking at the script and start looking at the state.

05:53.540 --> 05:55.500
That is the foundational shift right there.

05:55.600 --> 05:56.560
It's a philosophical pivot.

05:56.740 --> 05:57.200
How so?

05:57.320 --> 06:03.860
Well, if we ask a layperson, or frankly, even most novice software engineers, what is a program?

06:04.060 --> 06:04.360
Yeah.

06:04.480 --> 06:05.200
What do they say?

06:05.200 --> 06:08.380
The default answer they give is that it's the text.

06:08.840 --> 06:08.920
Right.

06:09.000 --> 06:09.920
The code on the screen.

06:10.100 --> 06:10.440
Exactly.

06:10.840 --> 06:12.920
It's the lines of code you type into a text editor.

06:13.200 --> 06:14.040
They have the recipe card.

06:14.240 --> 06:14.420
Okay.

06:14.700 --> 06:18.740
But the author argues forcefully that a program is absolutely not the text.

06:18.760 --> 06:19.160
It's not.

06:19.220 --> 06:20.840
No, the text is just a medium.

06:20.840 --> 06:26.380
The program itself is the actual behavior that the text produces out in the world.

06:26.500 --> 06:26.640
Okay.

06:26.740 --> 06:28.760
Let me make sure I'm really wrapping my head around this.

06:29.140 --> 06:36.360
So, if I'm thinking about a program as just the text I type, I'm focusing entirely on the instructions, not the outcome.

06:36.500 --> 06:36.720
Right.

06:36.880 --> 06:40.720
But the author is saying the outcome is the only thing that actually matters to the universe.

06:40.980 --> 06:41.420
Precisely.

06:41.740 --> 06:43.340
Let's bring this into the real world.

06:43.560 --> 06:43.980
Please.

06:43.980 --> 06:48.440
The author uses this brilliant analogy of two cooks baking a cake.

06:48.540 --> 06:49.340
I love this one.

06:49.460 --> 06:49.640
Yeah.

06:49.800 --> 06:51.600
So, we have Cook A and Cook B.

06:52.260 --> 06:54.280
They're both making a classic pound cake.

06:54.380 --> 06:54.520
Okay.

06:54.960 --> 06:56.040
Cook A is very traditional.

06:56.740 --> 07:03.300
They cream the butter and sugar together first, beat it until it's fluffy, and then they add the eggs one by one.

07:03.500 --> 07:03.760
Okay.

07:03.940 --> 07:04.160
Yeah.

07:04.220 --> 07:05.100
That's a standard method.

07:05.240 --> 07:05.460
Right.

07:05.460 --> 07:18.940
But then you have Cook B. Cook B decides to beat the eggs first, like in a totally separate bowl, until they're completely frothy, and then they fold those beaten eggs into the butter and sugar way later.

07:19.120 --> 07:23.140
So, the sequence of motions in the physical kitchen is completely different.

07:23.300 --> 07:30.260
If you were watching them on camera or reading a transcript of their physical actions, the scripts do not match at all.

07:30.580 --> 07:34.340
Cook A is doing action X, while Cook B is doing action Y.

07:34.340 --> 07:34.940
Exactly.

07:35.460 --> 07:38.160
Their transcripts are fundamentally entirely different.

07:38.220 --> 07:38.400
Right.

07:38.680 --> 07:42.960
But think about the diner who actually eats the finished cake at the end of the night.

07:43.080 --> 07:43.400
Okay.

07:44.000 --> 07:46.620
That diner is sitting at a table in the restaurant.

07:47.360 --> 07:48.380
They take a bite.

07:49.160 --> 07:54.220
Can they reverse engineer the exact order of those steps from the taste of the cake?

07:54.300 --> 07:55.640
No, of course not.

07:55.700 --> 07:55.920
Right.

07:56.020 --> 07:59.820
Assuming both methods actually work chemically, a pound cake is just a pound cake.

07:59.920 --> 08:00.320
Exactly.

08:00.460 --> 08:02.860
Both routes produce the exact same transformation.

08:02.860 --> 08:08.220
I mean, raw ingredients sitting on a counter were turned into a finished dessert sitting on a plate.

08:08.400 --> 08:10.160
And that right there is the crux of it.

08:10.380 --> 08:12.160
The diner only cares about the transition.

08:12.260 --> 08:12.900
The transition.

08:13.200 --> 08:13.400
Yes.

08:13.920 --> 08:18.060
The starting state was flour, sugar, butter, eggs.

08:18.560 --> 08:20.160
The ending state was a cake.

08:20.880 --> 08:27.480
The intermediate steps, the transcript of the program, they are completely irrelevant to the final state of the universe.

08:27.640 --> 08:28.640
That makes total sense.

08:28.640 --> 08:36.360
And this brings us to a formal concept the author leans on heavily, which is called observational equivalence.

08:37.000 --> 08:38.300
Observational equivalence.

08:38.640 --> 08:43.500
Meaning, if I observe the outcome, it is equivalent regardless of the path taken.

08:43.620 --> 08:43.900
Yes.

08:44.560 --> 08:49.360
Let's look at a mathematical example, because it strips away all the messiness of a physical kitchen.

08:49.540 --> 08:50.200
Always helpful.

08:50.200 --> 08:54.360
Suppose a math student is given a really basic equation to compute.

08:55.140 --> 08:58.500
Let's say two times the sum of three plus four.

08:59.100 --> 09:02.960
So, you know, a two outside parentheses and inside is three plus four.

09:03.080 --> 09:03.280
Right.

09:03.440 --> 09:06.140
I actually remember this from middle school algebra.

09:06.400 --> 09:06.780
Okay, good.

09:06.920 --> 09:08.460
You can follow the order of operations.

09:08.460 --> 09:10.600
So you do what's inside the parentheses first.

09:10.740 --> 09:10.860
Right.

09:10.860 --> 09:17.420
So you add the three and four to get seven, and then you just multiply that seven by the two on the outside to get 14.

09:17.560 --> 09:17.940
Exactly.

09:18.080 --> 09:18.740
That's path one.

09:19.060 --> 09:20.200
Now, what's path two?

09:20.400 --> 09:21.900
Well, you can distribute the two.

09:22.200 --> 09:22.480
Yes.

09:22.580 --> 09:25.140
You multiply the two by the three to get six.

09:25.440 --> 09:27.840
Then you multiply the two by the four to get eight.

09:28.000 --> 09:28.220
Right.

09:28.340 --> 09:31.900
Then you add the six and the eight together, and you still arrive at 14.

09:32.260 --> 09:33.460
The routes are entirely different.

09:34.140 --> 09:40.440
The intermediate states in the student's brain, and frankly, the intermediate pencil marks on the paper, are totally different.

09:40.440 --> 09:40.680
Right.

09:40.740 --> 09:42.220
They look different on the page.

09:42.420 --> 09:46.600
But the function from inputs to output does not change one bit.

09:46.780 --> 09:54.560
Both paths take the initial, the rod equation, and arrive at the exact same final state, which is 14.

09:54.820 --> 09:55.180
Exactly.

09:55.640 --> 10:02.440
If two different texts or transcripts realize the identical transformation, they're observationally equivalent.

10:02.680 --> 10:04.120
It reminds me of a thermostat.

10:04.260 --> 10:05.000
Oh, that's a good one.

10:05.000 --> 10:13.460
Yeah, because you could have a super cheap, old school, bimetallic strip thermostat running a really basic on-off algorithm.

10:13.660 --> 10:13.820
Right.

10:14.000 --> 10:24.660
Or you could have a $500 smart thermostat with a highly complex, you know, predictive, proportional, integral derivative controller analyzing local weather patterns and humidity.

10:24.880 --> 10:26.360
A whole computer on the wall.

10:26.360 --> 10:26.880
Exactly.

10:27.440 --> 10:35.900
But if they both take the room from 60 degrees to 70 degrees over the exact course of an hour, they have realized the exact same state transition.

10:36.260 --> 10:36.540
Yes.

10:37.060 --> 10:38.200
Or think about a train journey.

10:38.480 --> 10:44.520
You can take a completely different route, you know, switch tracks, hit entirely different intermediate stations.

10:44.700 --> 10:45.540
Take the scenic route.

10:45.780 --> 10:45.980
Right.

10:46.460 --> 10:51.980
But if you start in New York and end in Chicago, the macroscopic state transition is physically fixed.

10:51.980 --> 11:01.600
Okay, so if the program isn't the text, but the transition itself, how do we talk about this formally without just using metaphors forever?

11:01.740 --> 11:01.960
Right.

11:02.060 --> 11:03.620
We do need some formal grounding.

11:03.780 --> 11:06.100
The author introduces a bit of notation here, right?

11:06.440 --> 11:08.280
They use the Greek symbol sigma.

11:08.660 --> 11:09.460
Yes, sigma.

11:09.940 --> 11:13.620
Let's demystify that immediately because I know Greek letters can sound really intimidating.

11:13.860 --> 11:14.240
Definitely.

11:14.540 --> 11:17.620
Think of sigma simply as the state space.

11:17.760 --> 11:18.520
State space.

11:18.520 --> 11:18.920
Yes.

11:19.320 --> 11:24.540
It represents the complete set of all possible states a system could ever conceivably be in.

11:24.720 --> 11:25.380
Give me an example.

11:25.800 --> 11:31.640
Well, if your system is a chess board, sigma is every single legal arrangement of pieces on that board.

11:31.780 --> 11:32.060
Okay.

11:32.420 --> 11:43.760
If your system is a computer, sigma encompasses the value of every single variable, every position of a file cursor, the exact contents of the memory, even the physical temperature of the CPU.

11:43.760 --> 11:50.480
Wow. So sigma is just the entire universe of our specific problem at any frozen specific moment in time.

11:50.580 --> 11:52.280
Exactly. It's a snapshot of reality.

11:52.400 --> 11:53.040
Okay. I'm with you.

11:53.240 --> 12:01.240
A program then, formally speaking, is simply a function that maps an initial state, which we'll call lowercase sigma, to a resulting state.

12:01.540 --> 12:01.860
Okay.

12:01.860 --> 12:09.400
So a formal state transition is just moving from one specific confederation of sigma to another specific configuration of sigma.

12:09.880 --> 12:15.640
And in this context, when you define a function in a system, like when a programmer writes what they call a function signature,

12:16.020 --> 12:20.940
you are essentially writing a declared binding contract of this exact transition.

12:21.100 --> 12:22.240
Exactly. A contract.

12:22.240 --> 12:29.380
If you're saying to the system, if you hand me this specific state, I absolutely promise I will hand you back this specific resulting state.

12:29.520 --> 12:35.680
And that contract, that promise, is the entire foundation of how we build absolutely anything complex.

12:35.780 --> 12:36.940
Because we have to trust it.

12:37.180 --> 12:38.740
Right. It's what allows for composition.

12:38.980 --> 12:39.160
Right.

12:39.160 --> 12:51.040
Large systems, whether they're software architectures or physical supply chains or even bureaucratic governments, they are built by composing these small, predictable state transitions together.

12:51.040 --> 12:55.360
The assembly line analogy in the manuscript really drove this home for me.

12:55.720 --> 12:56.480
Walk us through it.

12:56.880 --> 13:00.500
So imagine you have a worker on a car assembly line, right?

13:01.020 --> 13:06.380
And their specific job, their only job, is to bolt on the front left wheel.

13:06.760 --> 13:07.020
Okay.

13:07.180 --> 13:11.120
That worker treats the supplier of the wheel as a total black box.

13:11.220 --> 13:14.380
A black box meaning they don't look inside it, they only look at the output.

13:14.580 --> 13:18.820
Exactly. The worker doesn't need to know what temperature the metal was forged at.

13:18.840 --> 13:19.080
Right.

13:19.080 --> 13:24.480
They don't need to know the name of the guy who poured the rubber or what specific brand of pont was used on the hubcap.

13:24.620 --> 13:25.020
They don't care.

13:25.260 --> 13:28.360
They just need to know that a wheel arrived at their station.

13:28.540 --> 13:28.720
Right.

13:28.800 --> 13:32.980
And it meets the specific geometric requirements to physically fit onto the axle.

13:33.180 --> 13:33.360
Right.

13:33.600 --> 13:38.880
The input state is axle plus loose wheel, and the output state is car with wheel attached.

13:39.220 --> 13:40.200
That's a perfect example.

13:40.880 --> 13:46.160
The system composes correctly because the worker trusts the declared state of the wheel.

13:46.280 --> 13:47.080
They trust the contract.

13:47.080 --> 13:50.420
Yes. Think about the Global Postal System.

13:50.500 --> 13:52.200
Oh, that's a huge system.

13:52.320 --> 13:54.020
It's a total miracle of composition.

13:54.560 --> 13:59.060
The system routes a letter based entirely on the address written on the outside of the envelope.

13:59.180 --> 13:59.380
Right.

13:59.780 --> 14:10.000
The massive, complex sorting machine doesn't open the envelope, read the contents, analyze the emotional tone of the letter, and then decide where it should logically go.

14:10.000 --> 14:12.160
Oh, you know, this looks like a messy breakup letter.

14:12.240 --> 14:13.720
I better send it to Chicago instead.

14:14.080 --> 14:14.180
Right.

14:14.260 --> 14:15.640
It would be completely absurd.

14:15.760 --> 14:16.840
And impossibly slow.

14:17.180 --> 14:17.660
Exactly.

14:17.960 --> 14:21.800
The system trusts the declared state, the address on the envelope.

14:22.040 --> 14:22.660
Nothing else.

14:22.940 --> 14:25.260
Or think of a municipal water treatment plant.

14:25.340 --> 14:25.600
Okay.

14:25.600 --> 14:36.860
The plant trusts that the water arriving from the first rough filtration stage actually meets the exact input requirements for the next stage, the chemical disinfection stage.

14:37.040 --> 14:38.020
Oh, I see.

14:38.100 --> 14:41.620
They don't retest the entire history of the water at every single pipe joint.

14:41.620 --> 14:44.840
They trust the transition contract of the previous stage.

14:44.840 --> 14:51.780
So we build big, towering things by chaining these small, predictable transitions together.

14:52.240 --> 14:54.520
Output of A becomes input of B.

14:54.740 --> 14:55.260
Exactly.

14:55.580 --> 15:02.120
But here is where it gets really interesting and where the author starts showing us how fragile this all is.

15:02.180 --> 15:03.220
The cracks in the foundation.

15:03.480 --> 15:03.720
Yeah.

15:04.340 --> 15:08.560
The author introduces this term, referential transparency.

15:09.100 --> 15:09.420
Yes.

15:09.420 --> 15:19.640
Because this whole beautiful, highly efficient chain of trust only works if a program's declared state actually captures absolutely everything it depends on.

15:19.780 --> 15:20.040
Exactly.

15:20.860 --> 15:28.820
Composition by type signature or by envelope address or by wheel specification is only trustworthy if that signature is the whole truth.

15:28.940 --> 15:30.480
The whole truth and nothing but the truth.

15:30.580 --> 15:30.800
Right.

15:30.880 --> 15:32.980
If it is an exhaustive list of dependencies.

15:33.640 --> 15:40.060
Referential transparency means that a program's output depends only on its declared inputs and absolutely nothing outside of that.

15:40.200 --> 15:41.800
Let me play devil's advocate here.

15:41.900 --> 15:42.320
Go for it.

15:42.440 --> 15:44.340
What happens if it's not the whole truth?

15:44.340 --> 15:52.780
Like, if the postal system suddenly started routing letters based on how heavy the ink inside the envelope was, but they didn't state that requirement on the outside.

15:52.840 --> 15:53.360
Okay, I follow.

15:53.500 --> 15:57.220
So if you use a heavy pen, it secretly goes to New York.

15:57.420 --> 16:00.720
And if you use a light pencil, it secretly goes to L.A.

16:00.720 --> 16:04.060
But the outside of both envelopes just says, send to Chicago.

16:04.220 --> 16:06.220
Well, the whole system breaks down instantly.

16:06.580 --> 16:06.900
Right.

16:06.980 --> 16:13.960
Because the user of the system, you, putting a stamp on the envelope, you are relying on the public contract.

16:14.380 --> 16:17.160
You provided the correct declared input.

16:17.280 --> 16:18.100
I followed the rules.

16:18.200 --> 16:18.580
You did.

16:19.200 --> 16:21.540
But the system had a hidden dependency, the ink weight.

16:22.320 --> 16:27.600
When a system relies on a hidden dependency, you completely lose the ability to predict its behavior.

16:27.780 --> 16:29.120
It's just random at that point.

16:29.120 --> 16:34.500
In software, this happens all the time when a program relies on ambient global variables.

16:34.700 --> 16:35.840
Give me an example of that.

16:36.060 --> 16:39.860
What does an ambient global variable actually look like in practice?

16:40.520 --> 16:44.340
Imagine a function in a checkout system that calculates sales tax.

16:44.500 --> 16:44.740
Okay.

16:45.000 --> 16:48.620
Its public signature says, give me your price and I'll give you the total.

16:48.880 --> 16:49.460
Simple enough.

16:49.560 --> 16:52.960
So you give it $10 and you expect it to return $10.50.

16:53.260 --> 16:54.760
Because of a 5% tax.

16:54.760 --> 16:55.400
Exactly.

16:55.780 --> 16:55.960
Okay.

16:56.300 --> 17:04.480
But internally, without telling you, it's reaching out to a hidden global variable in the overall system called current tax rate.

17:04.620 --> 17:06.660
Which isn't in its signature at all.

17:06.740 --> 17:09.080
It didn't ask for the tax rate as an input.

17:09.340 --> 17:09.860
Exactly.

17:10.080 --> 17:12.220
It's reaching outside its declared boundary.

17:12.440 --> 17:13.660
Sneaking around the back.

17:13.660 --> 17:13.940
Yeah.

17:14.000 --> 17:16.320
It is no longer referentially transparent.

17:16.940 --> 17:29.260
If someone else in the system accidentally changes current tax rate to, say, 20%, your function will suddenly return $12 instead of $10.50, even though you gave it the exact same $10 input.

17:29.380 --> 17:30.780
The contract lied to you.

17:30.880 --> 17:32.120
It absolutely lied.

17:32.240 --> 17:32.420
Wait.

17:32.720 --> 17:33.340
I love this.

17:33.400 --> 17:38.680
I want to jump in with a real-world analogy to make sure I am totally 100% grasping this.

17:38.720 --> 17:39.160
Let's hear it.

17:39.160 --> 17:42.660
Because this feels like a massive key to understanding the whole essay.

17:42.980 --> 17:43.440
It is.

17:43.620 --> 17:48.500
So, let's say I give my friend my grocery list and a crisp $20 bill.

17:48.680 --> 17:48.920
All right.

17:49.060 --> 17:53.120
The program is my friend taking the list and the money and returning with the food.

17:53.520 --> 17:55.440
The input is the list and the cash.

17:55.820 --> 17:57.260
The output is the groceries.

17:57.500 --> 17:57.620
Right.

17:57.620 --> 18:02.740
If they do exactly that, that is a referentially transparent transition.

18:03.380 --> 18:03.720
Correct.

18:04.240 --> 18:10.460
The declared list and the $20 captures absolutely everything needed for the transition to occur.

18:10.600 --> 18:14.000
What if I accidentally left my credit card sitting on the kitchen counter?

18:14.520 --> 18:15.840
Oh, I see where this is going.

18:16.000 --> 18:16.200
Yeah.

18:16.520 --> 18:18.940
My friend takes the list and the $20.

18:20.200 --> 18:26.420
But they get to the store, realize they want a fancy organic avocado, and the $20 isn't enough.

18:26.660 --> 18:26.860
Right.

18:26.860 --> 18:34.720
So, they reach into their pocket, pull out my credit card, which they grabbed off the counter without asking, and use it to cover the extra item.

18:34.960 --> 18:36.520
This is where it gets devious.

18:36.660 --> 18:37.000
Right.

18:37.200 --> 18:40.160
Because the transition looks identical to me when they walk in the front door.

18:40.320 --> 18:41.260
You can't tell the difference.

18:41.360 --> 18:41.940
They walk in.

18:42.000 --> 18:42.860
They hand me the groceries.

18:43.080 --> 18:47.640
It looks like a completely successful output based exactly on our contract.

18:47.780 --> 18:50.900
The footprint of their action was larger than the declared inputs.

18:51.180 --> 18:56.500
Is that why unsafe code in a language like Rust is considered so dangerous?

18:56.860 --> 18:57.680
That's the core of it.

18:57.680 --> 19:02.680
Because it's quietly using the credit card while pretending it only used the $20 bill.

19:02.880 --> 19:04.720
That is a phenomenal analogy.

19:05.120 --> 19:05.360
Yes.

19:05.600 --> 19:05.920
Awesome.

19:05.920 --> 19:12.980
When a function's real behavior silently exceeds its declared state space, the composition stops being reliable.

19:13.420 --> 19:14.960
You can't trust the chain anymore.

19:15.060 --> 19:15.240
Right.

19:15.620 --> 19:24.760
In a language that doesn't strictly enforce this, two identical calls to your grocery function could yield completely different macroscopic results.

19:24.760 --> 19:25.280
Right.

19:25.400 --> 19:28.000
The first time, you get groceries and lose $20.

19:28.600 --> 19:29.440
Standard transition.

19:29.580 --> 19:34.740
But the second time, you get groceries, but suddenly your credit card is maxed out and your mortgage bounces.

19:35.000 --> 19:39.980
Because the hidden global state, your credit card balance, was mutated without your knowledge.

19:40.220 --> 19:40.680
Wow.

19:40.860 --> 19:41.380
Precisely.

19:41.820 --> 19:47.540
Rust uses the unsafe keyword, not necessarily to say this code is broken and will immediately crash.

19:47.620 --> 19:48.800
Oh, it doesn't just mean broken.

19:48.800 --> 19:56.520
No, it's used as a highly visible admission that the function's actual dependency footprint has grown larger than its signature.

19:56.760 --> 19:57.600
It's a warning label.

19:57.900 --> 19:58.260
Exactly.

19:58.520 --> 20:00.860
It's a warning label for the compiler and the programmer.

20:01.920 --> 20:05.480
It says, referential transparency has been compromised right here.

20:05.720 --> 20:07.940
I am stepping outside the stated boundaries.

20:08.320 --> 20:09.660
I have the credit card.

20:09.840 --> 20:10.060
Right.

20:10.420 --> 20:12.700
You can no longer trust this component blindly.

20:13.060 --> 20:15.680
You must manually verify what it is touching.

20:15.960 --> 20:17.900
Okay, this is clicking for me now.

20:17.900 --> 20:26.820
A system is just a series of predictable state transitions, and we desperately need those transitions to be honest about what they touch.

20:27.100 --> 20:29.100
Honest transitions are everything.

20:29.440 --> 20:32.640
Which leads us perfectly into the next big question the author poses.

20:33.580 --> 20:40.000
If a system is just a chain of these predictable transitions, what is the single biggest threat to that predictability?

20:40.580 --> 20:42.540
Unseen changes happening underneath us.

20:42.620 --> 20:42.880
Yes.

20:43.220 --> 20:46.900
The author spends a huge amount of time talking about the danger of change.

20:46.900 --> 20:51.640
They talk about this deep, fundamental asymmetry between observation and mutation.

20:52.320 --> 20:55.700
This is a crucial distinction, honestly one that trips up a lot of system designers.

20:55.860 --> 20:56.200
How so?

20:56.540 --> 21:00.200
Well, we tend to lump accessing data into one big bucket.

21:00.560 --> 21:03.000
Like reading and writing are just access.

21:03.380 --> 21:03.800
Exactly.

21:04.420 --> 21:08.600
But looking at data and changing data are fundamentally opposite acts.

21:08.600 --> 21:13.480
Observation leaves other observers' beliefs entirely intact.

21:13.700 --> 21:14.320
Explain that.

21:14.400 --> 21:16.360
What do you mean by beliefs in this context?

21:16.360 --> 21:23.840
Think about a belief as a premise you hold to be true about the physical or digital world upon which you base your future action.

21:23.940 --> 21:24.080
Okay.

21:24.080 --> 21:29.200
If you and I are both standing in a train station looking at the huge departures board.

21:29.320 --> 21:29.620
Right.

21:29.620 --> 21:33.420
My act of looking at the board doesn't change the times you see.

21:33.460 --> 21:34.440
Oh, of course not.

21:34.440 --> 21:42.280
Any number of people, 10, 100, 1,000, can observe the same unchanging state and remain in perfect agreement indefinitely.

21:43.260 --> 21:47.700
We can all form beliefs based on that observation without ever interfering with each other.

21:47.840 --> 21:51.440
We all believe the train to Chicago leaves at 4.0 p.m.

21:51.480 --> 21:51.720
Right.

21:51.960 --> 21:52.700
But mutation.

21:52.960 --> 21:54.500
Mutation is a totally different beast.

21:54.620 --> 21:56.380
It's an assumption invalidating act.

21:56.440 --> 21:58.680
A bomb going off in the logic of the system.

21:58.680 --> 21:58.720
A bomb.

21:59.100 --> 21:59.520
Exactly.

21:59.700 --> 22:00.080
A bomb.

22:00.180 --> 22:02.480
A mutation doesn't just change the state.

22:02.940 --> 22:11.720
It silently invalidates every single belief held by every other party that was formed by observing the state before the mutation occurred.

22:12.060 --> 22:12.540
Wow.

22:12.900 --> 22:18.920
And it does this instantaneously, whether or not those parties are even aware the mutation happened.

22:19.040 --> 22:23.080
The library book analogy in the manuscript is so incredibly clear on this.

22:23.140 --> 22:24.340
Let's really walk through this one.

22:24.400 --> 22:25.200
That's a great visual.

22:25.200 --> 22:29.680
Imagine a rare historical manuscript in a library.

22:30.120 --> 22:37.300
If that library book only ever gets read, which is pure observation, it requires zero coordination from the librarian.

22:37.460 --> 22:37.680
None.

22:38.080 --> 22:40.420
Patron A can come in on Monday and read chapter one.

22:40.840 --> 22:43.220
Patron B can come in on Tuesday and read chapter one.

22:43.600 --> 22:46.420
A hundred people could read the exact same edition over a year.

22:46.560 --> 22:47.980
No one interferes with anyone else.

22:48.020 --> 22:50.040
Because reading is fundamentally non-destructive.

22:50.240 --> 22:51.740
It doesn't alter the state space.

22:51.740 --> 22:59.880
But the very second you let a patron annotate that book in ink, which is mutation, the entire calculus of the system just fractures.

22:59.960 --> 23:00.480
It shatters.

23:00.920 --> 23:03.760
Say Patron C comes in on Wednesday with a black pen.

23:04.300 --> 23:10.340
They decide a specific date in chapter one is wrong, so they cross out 1942 and write 1943.

23:11.000 --> 23:13.800
Now think about the logical fallout of that single act.

23:14.020 --> 23:14.720
It's huge.

23:15.320 --> 23:19.800
Every single future reader now has to manually verify if the text is trustworthy.

23:19.800 --> 23:20.600
Yes.

23:20.880 --> 23:22.640
Did the original author write 1943?

23:23.460 --> 23:24.840
Or did Patron C write it?

23:24.900 --> 23:27.840
Did someone cross out a not in a really critical sentence?

23:27.980 --> 23:28.460
I don't know.

23:28.740 --> 23:28.920
Right.

23:29.460 --> 23:34.560
And Patron A, who read it on Monday, now holds a belief that the date is 1942.

23:35.140 --> 23:37.740
That is completely out of sync with the current reality of the book.

23:37.780 --> 23:39.000
Their belief is now false.

23:39.280 --> 23:43.320
The mutation changed what every subsequent reader is now obligated to check.

23:43.680 --> 23:46.800
Think about how this applies to our digital lives every day.

23:46.900 --> 23:47.620
Give me an example.

23:47.620 --> 23:49.680
A shared team calendar at work.

23:49.780 --> 23:51.040
Oh, I know this pain.

23:51.380 --> 23:51.680
Right.

23:51.820 --> 23:52.000
Yeah.

23:52.060 --> 23:54.740
If the calendar is just being viewed by everyone on the team, it's fine.

23:54.800 --> 23:55.940
It's a solid source of truth.

23:56.040 --> 23:56.180
Yeah.

23:56.680 --> 24:02.660
But a single mutation by one person can silently double book a conference room.

24:02.820 --> 24:03.660
Literally the worst.

24:04.040 --> 24:09.700
The viewer who checked the calendar an hour ago now has a prior belief that the room is empty.

24:10.380 --> 24:14.280
But that belief is now a live source of errors.

24:14.460 --> 24:15.520
They're walking into a trap.

24:15.520 --> 24:20.200
They're going to walk into that room with an important client and someone else will be sitting there.

24:20.600 --> 24:22.520
Or a restaurant menu updating mid-service.

24:23.000 --> 24:25.000
This is literally my nightmare scenario.

24:25.120 --> 24:26.340
Oh, that's a brilliant example.

24:26.520 --> 24:27.920
You sit down at a nice restaurant.

24:28.260 --> 24:29.620
You look at the physical menu.

24:29.920 --> 24:31.000
You observe the state.

24:31.280 --> 24:32.180
You form a belief.

24:32.180 --> 24:32.700
Exactly.

24:32.900 --> 24:34.180
You decide you really want the salmon.

24:34.340 --> 24:35.080
You form a belief.

24:35.420 --> 24:36.520
I will eat salmon tonight.

24:37.580 --> 24:38.720
The waiter comes over.

24:39.200 --> 24:47.540
But silently, five seconds prior, the kitchen just updated their digital point of sale system to say they are completely out of salmon.

24:47.840 --> 24:48.740
What happens to your order?

24:48.740 --> 24:58.120
Your order, which was totally logically sound based on your observation of the old state, is now resting on an assumption the kitchen absolutely cannot honor.

24:58.380 --> 24:58.560
Yep.

24:58.880 --> 25:02.940
You place the order, the waiter punches it in, and the system just rejects it.

25:03.040 --> 25:07.960
What's fascinating here is that the author points out the cost of these mutations isn't flat.

25:08.460 --> 25:08.940
What do you mean?

25:09.320 --> 25:11.480
The manuscript introduces this concept.

25:12.120 --> 25:14.320
The cost of a mutation scales with reachability.

25:14.460 --> 25:14.900
Reachability.

25:15.060 --> 25:16.040
Okay, let's unpack that.

25:16.140 --> 25:20.280
Because it sounds like a telecom term, what does it actually mean in this context?

25:20.760 --> 25:25.540
Reachability is simply a measure of how many distinct paths can access a specific value.

25:25.760 --> 25:26.060
Okay.

25:26.060 --> 25:31.860
How many people, or how many parts of a program, have a direct line of sight to the thing you want to change.

25:31.960 --> 25:32.320
Got it.

25:32.560 --> 25:36.440
If only you have a copy of the menu, and you're the only one eating in the whole restaurant,

25:36.440 --> 25:39.600
the kitchen changing it is a highly localized problem.

25:39.880 --> 25:40.920
They just come out and tell you.

25:41.120 --> 25:42.600
The reachability is just one.

25:42.700 --> 25:43.000
Exactly.

25:43.880 --> 25:49.100
But what if that menu is broadcast to a thousand people across a city on a food delivery app?

25:49.240 --> 25:49.940
Oh, wow.

25:50.360 --> 25:55.320
Changing an item silently invalidates a thousand potential assumptions simultaneously.

25:55.780 --> 25:57.320
That's a huge blast radius.

25:57.380 --> 25:57.720
Yes.

25:58.400 --> 26:03.920
The more paths that can reach a value, the more observers whose beliefs must be accounted for

26:03.920 --> 26:06.660
before a mutation can be considered truly safe.

26:06.900 --> 26:07.160
Okay.

26:07.280 --> 26:14.200
If you have high reachability, and you allow uncoordinated mutation, you have guaranteed chaos.

26:14.440 --> 26:20.580
So, if a mutation happens, and people are relying on the old information, the system fails.

26:21.300 --> 26:23.560
The beliefs are out of sync with reality.

26:23.700 --> 26:23.960
Correct.

26:23.960 --> 26:28.260
To fix this, the author says we have to look at the concept of locality.

26:28.740 --> 26:30.760
They call it bounded reasoning footprints.

26:31.620 --> 26:35.620
Locality is the ultimate antidote to the reachability problem.

26:35.900 --> 26:36.700
How does it work?

26:37.220 --> 26:42.960
Locality means that when you are trying to verify a property of a system, by asking,

26:43.460 --> 26:44.960
is it safe to change this tire?

26:45.800 --> 26:48.380
Or, is it safe to delete this file?

26:49.380 --> 26:53.060
You only need to look at a bounded, finite neighborhood of that system.

26:53.240 --> 26:54.720
You don't have to look everywhere.

26:54.880 --> 26:56.440
You do not need to look at the whole universe.

26:56.660 --> 26:56.860
Right.

26:56.980 --> 27:01.120
Like, a mathematician proving a lemma, which is like a small stepping stone theorem.

27:01.280 --> 27:02.040
Perfect example.

27:02.240 --> 27:06.080
Once that lemma is proven to be true within its own little local logical rules,

27:06.240 --> 27:09.720
they don't have to hold all of mathematics in their head to use it later in a much bigger proof.

27:09.860 --> 27:10.380
No, they don't.

27:10.460 --> 27:11.300
The proof is bounded.

27:11.480 --> 27:12.820
The locality is tight.

27:12.940 --> 27:16.100
Or a mechanic replacing a worn serpentine bolt on a car engine.

27:16.660 --> 27:18.180
Think about the physical locality of that.

27:18.180 --> 27:22.620
Yeah, they open the hood, they release the tensioner, they swap the belt.

27:22.780 --> 27:23.200
That's it.

27:23.320 --> 27:27.940
They don't have to completely disassemble the entire car, they don't check the exhaust pipe,

27:28.020 --> 27:31.620
they don't take apart the automatic transmission, they don't test the windshield wipers,

27:31.760 --> 27:33.860
just to confirm the new belt works.

27:33.940 --> 27:37.900
No, the interface of the belt to the engine is completely local.

27:38.120 --> 27:43.800
If we connect this to the bigger picture, locality is basically how human civilization scales.

27:43.960 --> 27:44.460
Oh, absolutely.

27:44.460 --> 27:48.140
You understand your specific city block's zoning.

27:48.920 --> 27:52.460
You know this side of the street is residential and that side is commercial.

27:53.080 --> 27:58.100
You can make local decisions about opening a lemonade stand based purely on that bounded knowledge.

27:58.240 --> 28:03.220
You do not need to model the entire municipal water and drainage system for the whole county

28:03.220 --> 28:04.700
just to know if you can sell lemonade.

28:05.180 --> 28:06.100
That would be paralyzing.

28:06.100 --> 28:07.540
You make local decisions.

28:07.980 --> 28:08.640
Lego bricks.

28:09.000 --> 28:11.640
That was my absolute favorite example in the whole manuscript.

28:11.880 --> 28:12.740
Oh, the Legos, yes.

28:13.280 --> 28:16.040
Lego studs connect using only their local interface.

28:16.280 --> 28:18.900
The little bumps on top, the little tubes on the bottom.

28:19.300 --> 28:24.000
When you click two bricks together, you don't need to know if the top brick is eventually going

28:24.000 --> 28:28.360
to be part of a pirate ship mast or if the bottom brick is part of a castle wall.

28:28.540 --> 28:29.360
You don't care at all.

28:29.520 --> 28:30.800
You just look at the studs.

28:31.040 --> 28:34.580
The reasoning footprint is exactly the size of a two by four brick.

28:34.740 --> 28:36.600
But imagine a world without locality.

28:36.720 --> 28:37.640
Sounds terrifying.

28:37.800 --> 28:42.780
When a system lacks locality, when any part of the system can silently mutate data

28:42.780 --> 28:48.500
that another totally remote part relies on, you get what software engineers call action

28:48.500 --> 28:49.300
at a distance.

28:49.480 --> 28:50.520
Action at a distance.

28:50.940 --> 28:54.440
Like spooky quantum entanglement, but for software bugs.

28:54.600 --> 28:55.240
Precisely.

28:55.680 --> 29:02.120
If a property is only globally zerifiable meaning, to know if your one tiny function is safe to

29:02.120 --> 29:07.720
run, you have to search the entire millions of lines code base to make sure no one else

29:07.720 --> 29:09.880
is currently mutating the data you want to read.

29:10.040 --> 29:12.460
The system becomes fundamentally unmaintainable.

29:12.460 --> 29:13.180
It's impossible.

29:13.580 --> 29:16.320
It's like trying to fix the plumbing under your kitchen sink.

29:16.700 --> 29:22.160
But because the city's plumbing has zero locality, you have to check every single faucet in the

29:22.160 --> 29:27.840
entire city simultaneously to make sure a pressure spike from someone flushing a toilet five miles

29:27.840 --> 29:29.700
away doesn't ruin your repair.

29:30.020 --> 29:33.100
Which is obviously impossible for a human brain to compute.

29:33.160 --> 29:34.240
Completely impossible.

29:34.500 --> 29:34.820
Exactly.

29:35.800 --> 29:41.080
This is where the specific genius of Rust's aliasing rules comes in.

29:41.080 --> 29:42.400
Okay, let's talk about aliasing.

29:42.860 --> 29:46.600
Aliasing just means having multiple paths to the exact same data.

29:47.560 --> 29:51.780
Rust physically bounds the reasoning footprint to the function signature alone.

29:51.940 --> 29:53.600
So it forces locality.

29:53.760 --> 29:54.060
Yes.

29:54.720 --> 30:00.360
It says structurally, at the compiler level, I guarantee absolutely no one else in the entire

30:00.360 --> 30:03.760
universe is mutating this data while you are looking at it.

30:03.760 --> 30:04.780
Wait, hold on.

30:04.900 --> 30:08.040
Let me push back on this or at least ask a question here that I think a lot of people listening

30:08.040 --> 30:08.640
might be thinking.

30:08.840 --> 30:09.060
Sure.

30:09.280 --> 30:11.000
What about garbage collected languages?

30:11.400 --> 30:13.480
Things like Python, Java, JavaScript.

30:13.480 --> 30:19.140
Most people think of those as safe languages precisely because you don't have to manually manage memory.

30:19.540 --> 30:24.580
Doesn't the garbage collector solve this action at a distance mutation problem for us?

30:24.580 --> 30:30.780
I am so glad you brought that up because that raises a brilliant and deeply, deeply misunderstood

30:30.780 --> 30:32.680
distinction in computer science.

30:32.800 --> 30:33.280
Oh, really?

30:33.500 --> 30:33.760
Yes.

30:34.280 --> 30:37.120
No, garbage collected languages do not solve this problem.

30:37.260 --> 30:38.120
Not even a little bit.

30:38.120 --> 30:38.680
Really?

30:38.960 --> 30:41.500
But they are literally called safe languages.

30:41.740 --> 30:44.520
They are memory safe, not logically safe.

30:44.640 --> 30:45.500
Okay, break that down.

30:46.180 --> 30:48.300
Let's look at what a garbage collector actually does.

30:48.960 --> 30:51.880
A garbage collector keeps memory from being deleted too early.

30:52.120 --> 30:52.400
Right.

30:52.840 --> 30:55.240
It solves what is called the use after free problem.

30:56.180 --> 31:01.340
It ensures that if you are holding a reference to an object, the underlying physical memory won't

31:01.340 --> 31:02.740
just vanish out from under you.

31:02.860 --> 31:04.940
It ensures the data physically exists.

31:05.080 --> 31:05.500
Exactly.

31:05.500 --> 31:10.280
But a garbage collector does absolutely nothing to stop action at a distance mutation.

31:10.600 --> 31:11.340
Oh, wait.

31:11.540 --> 31:15.180
So it just guarantees the object is there, but not what state the object is in.

31:15.460 --> 31:15.980
Exactly.

31:16.740 --> 31:21.720
Two completely unrelated threads in a garbage collected language, say one thread handling

31:21.720 --> 31:27.200
a user login and another thread handling a database backup, they can still hold a reference

31:27.200 --> 31:30.740
to the exact same object and mutate it simultaneously.

31:30.880 --> 31:31.580
Oh, wow.

31:31.840 --> 31:33.200
This creates a data race.

31:33.200 --> 31:35.900
It silently invalidates assumptions.

31:36.440 --> 31:41.380
A garbage collector manages reachability for deallocation, but it does not manage authority

31:41.380 --> 31:42.660
over mutation.

31:42.880 --> 31:45.560
Let me use the hospital analogy again to see if I have this right.

31:45.620 --> 31:45.980
Go for it.

31:45.980 --> 31:49.860
So the garbage collector just makes sure the x-ray machine is physically plugged into the

31:49.860 --> 31:51.800
wall and hasn't been thrown in the dumpster outback.

31:51.880 --> 31:52.100
Right.

31:52.100 --> 31:57.500
It manages existence, but it doesn't stop two different doctors from fighting over the controls,

31:57.760 --> 32:03.040
pressing buttons at the exact same time, and exposing the film twice, ruining the entire

32:03.040 --> 32:03.440
image.

32:03.540 --> 32:04.720
That is exactly it.

32:04.720 --> 32:11.380
It prevents a hard crash from accessing deleted memory, but it doesn't prevent logical chaos.

32:11.760 --> 32:13.760
The doctors aren't coordinating their authority.

32:13.900 --> 32:16.520
Which brings us perfectly to the core of the author's argument.

32:16.980 --> 32:20.300
We've established that unseen mutation destroys local reasoning.

32:20.920 --> 32:23.520
We desperately need to be able to trust what we observe.

32:23.520 --> 32:29.760
So how does the system actually enforce this locality in a shared environment without a garbage

32:29.760 --> 32:30.180
collector?

32:30.440 --> 32:37.460
The author says, by rigidly and flexibly controlling who has the authority to make a change.

32:37.740 --> 32:37.960
Right.

32:38.220 --> 32:42.980
And to deeply understand this, we have to look at what happens when you actually possess a

32:42.980 --> 32:43.460
resource.

32:44.320 --> 32:49.600
Every object, every resource, every piece of state has what the author calls admissible

32:49.600 --> 32:50.520
continuations.

32:50.860 --> 32:51.900
Admissible continuations.

32:51.900 --> 32:55.220
Meaning the valid things that can logically happen next.

32:55.360 --> 32:55.560
Yes.

32:56.040 --> 33:01.920
The operations that can validly extend a resource's history without producing a logical contradiction.

33:02.300 --> 33:03.040
Give me an example.

33:03.340 --> 33:08.140
If a file on your computer is currently in a closed state, an admissible continuation might

33:08.140 --> 33:10.680
be to reopen it or to delete it or to rename it.

33:10.720 --> 33:10.980
Sure.

33:11.300 --> 33:14.600
But reading data from a closed file is not an admissible continuation.

33:15.020 --> 33:16.980
It violates the fundamental logic of the state.

33:17.040 --> 33:18.100
You can't unring a bell.

33:18.320 --> 33:20.580
You can't bake an egg after you've already scrambled it.

33:20.580 --> 33:23.820
The laws of physics dictate the admissible continuations.

33:24.140 --> 33:24.680
Exactly.

33:25.740 --> 33:31.360
So how do we systematically track which continuations are admissible in a purely digital system?

33:31.720 --> 33:32.600
How does Rust do it?

33:32.980 --> 33:38.500
Rust's incredible insight is that you can determine this by tracking one specific thing.

33:39.780 --> 33:40.980
Uniqueness of authority.

33:41.220 --> 33:42.260
Uniqueness of authority.

33:42.260 --> 33:43.840
The core rule is this.

33:44.720 --> 33:47.040
Ownership equals uniqueness of authority.

33:47.820 --> 33:53.780
At any given moment in time, there is exactly one place in the system authorized to decide

33:53.780 --> 33:55.380
what happens next to a value.

33:55.780 --> 33:58.580
The master key held by one guard.

33:58.720 --> 33:59.020
Yes.

33:59.100 --> 34:01.500
Let's say a high security vault has a master key.

34:01.500 --> 34:07.180
If there is literally only one physical copy of that key in existence and only one guard

34:07.180 --> 34:11.200
holds it, there is never any confusion about who is authorizing entry.

34:11.320 --> 34:12.280
No confusion at all.

34:12.440 --> 34:14.940
You don't have to check a database to see who opened the door.

34:15.040 --> 34:17.180
You just look at who is physically holding the key.

34:17.280 --> 34:17.460
Right.

34:17.600 --> 34:19.780
And if someone else wants to open the door, what has to happen?

34:19.840 --> 34:21.760
The guard has to physically hand the key to them.

34:21.840 --> 34:22.120
Yes.

34:22.260 --> 34:26.540
And the moment they hand it over, the original guard can no longer open the door.

34:26.700 --> 34:27.760
They gave up their authority.

34:27.940 --> 34:28.360
It's gone.

34:28.360 --> 34:31.580
The author also uses the analogy of a relay race baton.

34:32.560 --> 34:37.460
The rules of a track and field relay race dictate that exactly one runner holds the baton

34:37.460 --> 34:38.120
at a time.

34:38.300 --> 34:39.020
Exactly one.

34:39.220 --> 34:43.640
The moment you pass the baton to the next runner, the rules strictly dictate that you

34:43.640 --> 34:44.740
cannot touch it anymore.

34:45.140 --> 34:50.140
You assign ownership to someone else, and the physics of the baton enforce that you no longer

34:50.140 --> 34:50.840
have authority.

34:51.400 --> 34:53.680
You can't just run alongside them and hold the other end.

34:53.940 --> 34:55.880
A rental car is another really great example.

34:55.940 --> 34:56.360
Oh, yeah.

34:56.360 --> 35:01.880
There is exactly one active renter authorized to drive that specific car off the lot at

35:01.880 --> 35:02.280
a time.

35:02.620 --> 35:05.160
The rental agency's paperwork guarantees it.

35:05.460 --> 35:05.560
Right.

35:05.760 --> 35:10.320
When you assign ownership in Rust, the compiler acts like that strict paperwork.

35:11.180 --> 35:15.600
It rigidly enforces that the previous owner can no longer touch the value.

35:15.860 --> 35:21.000
If you hand a variable to another function, you can no longer read it, write to it, or delete

35:21.000 --> 35:21.220
it.

35:21.560 --> 35:22.920
You are entirely done with it.

35:22.920 --> 35:26.720
But wait, if we apply that strictly, wouldn't that bring a system to a grinding halt?

35:27.440 --> 35:27.880
What do you mean?

35:27.880 --> 35:32.320
If we had to pass the rental car title back and forth just to let someone look at the paint

35:32.320 --> 35:34.860
color of the car, nothing would ever get done.

35:34.940 --> 35:36.320
It would be incredibly inefficient.

35:36.420 --> 35:37.160
It would be a nightmare.

35:37.560 --> 35:40.980
Which is why the author introduces this crucial separation.

35:41.780 --> 35:44.580
They separate authority from knowledge.

35:44.960 --> 35:45.380
Ah, yes.

35:45.380 --> 35:46.860
This is where borrowing comes in.

35:47.080 --> 35:51.400
The manuscript defines borrowing as knowledge without authority.

35:51.820 --> 35:53.520
This is a profound distinction.

35:54.040 --> 35:58.000
And it's how you make a strict system actually usable in the real world.

35:58.560 --> 36:02.000
You can grant temporary, non-exclusive observation.

36:02.220 --> 36:02.840
How does that look?

36:02.980 --> 36:05.980
Think about looking at a museum exhibit behind thick glass.

36:06.140 --> 36:06.420
Okay.

36:06.420 --> 36:11.380
Anyone can walk up, look at the dinosaur bones, and gain perfect, complete knowledge of what

36:11.380 --> 36:14.340
the bones look like, how big they are, what species they belong to.

36:14.380 --> 36:15.540
They possess knowledge.

36:15.760 --> 36:16.040
Yes.

36:16.420 --> 36:20.440
But the glass prevents them from having any authority over the bones.

36:20.700 --> 36:23.360
They cannot reach in and rearrange a femur.

36:23.560 --> 36:24.380
Reading a newspaper.

36:24.560 --> 36:24.780
Yes.

36:24.780 --> 36:26.080
I can read the front page.

36:26.200 --> 36:27.320
You can read the front page.

36:27.820 --> 36:31.400
Millions of people can read it simultaneously across the entire country.

36:31.560 --> 36:33.520
We all have perfect knowledge of the headlines.

36:33.960 --> 36:34.740
Perfect knowledge.

36:34.740 --> 36:38.740
But none of us have the authority to actually edit tomorrow's edition.

36:39.020 --> 36:41.260
We are just borrowing the state of the news.

36:41.480 --> 36:46.460
And because pure observation doesn't change what the value's next legitimate continuation

36:46.460 --> 36:49.360
could be, I mean, reading the news doesn't change the news.

36:49.420 --> 36:49.640
Right.

36:49.760 --> 36:53.400
The system permits an arbitrary number of shared observers to coexist.

36:54.400 --> 36:59.140
Ten people reading an unchanging value cannot possibly produce an inconsistency.

36:59.140 --> 37:04.380
It is fundamentally safe to share knowledge, as long as absolutely no one has authority.

37:04.740 --> 37:06.600
But then what if something does need to be changed?

37:06.920 --> 37:07.200
Right.

37:07.400 --> 37:08.620
Eventually things have to change.

37:08.760 --> 37:13.400
What if the museum curator notices a dinosaur bone is loose and needs to fix it?

37:14.040 --> 37:15.500
We have to upgrade from just looking.

37:15.620 --> 37:16.520
We need authority.

37:16.880 --> 37:17.440
Yes, we do.

37:17.980 --> 37:20.860
The manuscript calls this mutable borrowing.

37:21.520 --> 37:25.680
And mutable borrowing is strictly defined as exclusive authority.

37:25.680 --> 37:27.480
This is where the rubber meets the road.

37:28.060 --> 37:31.760
To mutate a value is to actively redirect its continuation.

37:32.460 --> 37:33.360
It changes the future.

37:33.760 --> 37:38.980
As we discussed earlier with the library book, if two independent parts of a program were permitted

37:38.980 --> 37:44.600
to mutate the same value at the exact same moment, each would silently invalidate the other.

37:45.680 --> 37:49.800
So, Rust grants temporary exclusive authority.

37:50.040 --> 37:50.820
Like a surgeon.

37:51.000 --> 37:53.240
This was my favorite analogy for mutable borrowing.

37:53.420 --> 37:53.980
It's so vivid.

37:53.980 --> 37:59.860
When a surgeon is operating on a patient, they have exclusive authority over that patient's

37:59.860 --> 38:00.600
physical body.

38:00.960 --> 38:01.060
Right.

38:01.500 --> 38:05.980
The medical students and observers in the gallery up above can watch through the glass.

38:06.420 --> 38:07.540
They have shared knowledge.

38:07.800 --> 38:08.520
They are observing.

38:08.940 --> 38:12.620
But everyone in the physical room has to stand totally clear.

38:13.180 --> 38:14.700
Only the surgeon holds the scalpel.

38:14.900 --> 38:15.320
Exactly.

38:15.560 --> 38:17.300
Only the surgeon can make a change.

38:17.540 --> 38:21.800
If an observer suddenly rushed in and tried to help make an incision, it would be an absolute

38:21.800 --> 38:22.380
disaster.

38:22.380 --> 38:25.840
A mechanic fixing a heavy industrial machine is another one.

38:26.480 --> 38:29.260
Have you ever seen a lockout-tagout procedure in a factory?

38:29.420 --> 38:32.180
Yes, where they put the physical padlock on the power switch.

38:32.260 --> 38:32.560
Exactly.

38:32.760 --> 38:36.800
You lock out the power, put your specific tag on it, and everyone stands clear while the

38:36.800 --> 38:37.520
mechanic works.

38:37.640 --> 38:37.860
Right.

38:38.080 --> 38:41.140
The mechanic has exclusive authority over the machine state.

38:41.140 --> 38:46.520
While they are mutating, taking it apart, no one else is allowed to even observe it by

38:46.520 --> 38:47.180
turning it on.

38:47.280 --> 38:48.200
Because it would kill them.

38:48.360 --> 38:48.740
Exactly.

38:48.980 --> 38:51.480
Or think of a bank teller auditing a cash drawer.

38:52.180 --> 38:57.100
While that drawer is open and being counted by the teller, no other teller can reach into

38:57.100 --> 38:57.320
it.

38:57.660 --> 39:01.600
The exclusivity is absolute, but crucially, it is temporary.

39:01.600 --> 39:06.160
It is scoped tightly to the duration of the act that actually requires it.

39:06.160 --> 39:07.960
And the compiler enforces this.

39:08.200 --> 39:09.860
It acts as the laws of physics.

39:10.200 --> 39:14.320
It says you can have a million readers, or you can have exactly one writer.

39:14.540 --> 39:14.980
Whoever both.

39:15.000 --> 39:17.680
But you can never, ever have both at the same time.

39:18.040 --> 39:20.540
And this authority has a strict time limit.

39:20.660 --> 39:22.840
This is what lifetimes are in the language, right?

39:23.120 --> 39:23.300
Yes.

39:23.300 --> 39:26.960
They aren't tracking time like a stopwatch ticking down seconds.

39:27.280 --> 39:29.480
They are proofs of a validity span.

39:29.600 --> 39:30.000
Exactly.

39:30.740 --> 39:35.000
An observation is only meaningful for as long as the value it points to remains alive.

39:35.360 --> 39:40.600
If the museum exhibit is packed up and shipped to Paris, your observation in New York is no

39:40.600 --> 39:41.280
longer valid.

39:42.220 --> 39:45.380
A lifetime is a compile time proof obligation.

39:46.280 --> 39:52.300
It mathematically guarantees that an observation doesn't outlive the actual thing being observed.

39:52.300 --> 39:55.000
A visitor pass that expires at 5 p.m.

39:55.140 --> 39:55.460
Yes.

39:55.680 --> 39:58.340
If you are a contractor in a corporate office, you get a badge.

39:58.760 --> 40:02.780
If you try to swipe that badge at 6 p.m., the system rejects it.

40:02.880 --> 40:03.740
The door doesn't open.

40:04.160 --> 40:07.360
The underlying resource, your right to be in the building, has ended.

40:07.620 --> 40:08.140
It's gone.

40:08.420 --> 40:10.540
A temporary parking permit on your dashboard.

40:11.000 --> 40:15.600
A hotel key card that mathematically deactivates the moment you check out at 11 a.m.

40:15.960 --> 40:17.540
The access was always bounded.

40:17.980 --> 40:20.260
The expiration just makes it visible to the system.

40:20.260 --> 40:24.260
What's really fascinating here is how people interpret this psychologically.

40:24.600 --> 40:24.920
What do you mean?

40:25.400 --> 40:30.440
Well, many developers encounter the borrow checker in Rust, you know, the part of the

40:30.440 --> 40:34.300
compiler that strictly enforces these rules, and they find it incredibly frustrating.

40:34.540 --> 40:35.780
Oh, I've heard the complaints.

40:35.880 --> 40:39.780
They see it as a linter, just an overzealous style guy that is constantly nagging them.

40:39.820 --> 40:42.300
They think, I know what I'm doing, just let me compile the code.

40:42.600 --> 40:43.580
Get out of my way.

40:43.720 --> 40:43.980
Right.

40:43.980 --> 40:45.660
But it is not a style preference.

40:45.900 --> 40:48.320
It is a static admissibility verifier.

40:49.120 --> 40:51.040
Here is where it gets really interesting to me.

40:51.800 --> 40:54.100
People complain about the borrow checker being annoying.

40:55.040 --> 41:00.780
But isn't it just a compiler acting like a massive bouncer at a club, strictly enforcing

41:00.780 --> 41:02.360
the physics of time and space?

41:02.540 --> 41:03.880
A bouncer, exactly.

41:03.880 --> 41:09.140
If two runners try to grab the relay baton at the exact same moment, the bouncer steps

41:09.140 --> 41:09.380
in.

41:09.940 --> 41:11.300
It's not nagging you.

41:11.560 --> 41:15.080
It's physically stopping you from breaking the fundamental laws of reality.

41:15.580 --> 41:18.160
It's preventing you from being two places at once.

41:18.300 --> 41:19.460
That is precisely it.

41:19.620 --> 41:21.600
And there is a beautiful paradox here, actually.

41:21.780 --> 41:22.140
What is it?

41:22.360 --> 41:27.600
By subtracting a freedom, the freedom to have unrestricted, uncoordinated aliasing and mutation,

41:27.740 --> 41:30.120
you know, the freedom to just do whatever you want, whenever you want.

41:30.220 --> 41:30.400
Right.

41:30.400 --> 41:35.360
It actually creates the foundation for something totally new, fearless concurrency.

41:35.820 --> 41:36.720
Fearless concurrency.

41:36.840 --> 41:41.520
You can write incredibly complex, multi-threaded systems doing thousands of things at once

41:41.520 --> 41:47.120
without any fear of data races, because the bouncer has proven, structurally, that a collision

41:47.120 --> 41:48.620
is physically impossible.

41:49.120 --> 41:49.520
Wow.

41:49.720 --> 41:51.020
The constraint is generative.

41:51.400 --> 41:55.160
It frees your mind from having to ever worry about action at a distance again.

41:55.300 --> 41:56.520
It doesn't just stop bugs.

41:56.900 --> 41:58.500
It generates new possibilities.

41:58.500 --> 42:01.200
I absolutely love that way of framing it.

42:01.360 --> 42:02.140
It changes everything.

42:02.440 --> 42:07.460
So we've secured the data from being changed underneath us by aggressively managing who

42:07.460 --> 42:08.200
has authority.

42:08.740 --> 42:11.340
But now we have to ask a totally different question.

42:11.520 --> 42:11.740
Okay.

42:12.160 --> 42:15.240
How do we correctly define what that data is in the first place?

42:15.620 --> 42:18.000
How do we build the shapes of reality?

42:18.260 --> 42:19.220
This is the fun part.

42:19.420 --> 42:22.400
This brings us to the next massive shift in thinking.

42:23.120 --> 42:23.980
Shaping state.

42:25.080 --> 42:27.420
Structs, enums, and reconstruction.

42:27.420 --> 42:27.900
Right.

42:28.260 --> 42:31.080
We have managed authority, but authority over what exactly?

42:31.520 --> 42:33.900
We have to look at the shape of the state space itself.

42:34.100 --> 42:34.420
Okay.

42:34.700 --> 42:39.200
How do we carve out a valid piece of reality from the infinite possibilities of the universe?

42:39.920 --> 42:44.480
The author begins with structs, defining them formally as product state spaces.

42:44.640 --> 42:45.400
Product state spaces.

42:45.540 --> 42:45.740
Okay.

42:45.960 --> 42:46.600
Math term again.

42:46.680 --> 42:48.440
What does that actually mean in human terms?

42:48.440 --> 42:54.940
It means a struct's fields declare exactly what a value is allowed to become by demanding

42:54.940 --> 42:57.260
all of its fields simultaneously, right?

42:57.580 --> 42:57.880
Yes.

42:58.680 --> 43:01.840
A struct is a Cartesian product of its fields.

43:02.060 --> 43:04.680
And we really need to be very precise here.

43:04.760 --> 43:05.000
Okay.

43:05.180 --> 43:05.620
Lay it on me.

43:05.820 --> 43:10.680
A struct is not merely a convenient container like, you know, a digital Ziploc bag where you

43:10.680 --> 43:12.080
just toss related variables.

43:12.160 --> 43:12.380
Right.

43:12.380 --> 43:13.820
It's not just a bag.

43:13.960 --> 43:14.080
No.

43:14.280 --> 43:21.240
It is a hard mathematical boundary declaring the complete exhaustive space of values that

43:21.240 --> 43:23.500
could ever count as an instance of it.

43:23.980 --> 43:25.640
It's an A and D relationship.

43:25.840 --> 43:26.280
A and D.

43:26.360 --> 43:31.760
To exist, this thing must have field A and D field, B and D field C.

43:31.940 --> 43:33.480
The passport application form.

43:33.500 --> 43:33.800
Perfect.

43:34.100 --> 43:37.840
When you fill out a passport application, it has very specific fields.

43:37.840 --> 43:42.660
It requires a given name and a date of birth and a place of birth and a photograph.

43:42.940 --> 43:43.620
You can't skip one.

43:43.780 --> 43:46.600
If you bring a form with no photograph, the clerk rejects it.

43:46.660 --> 43:48.800
It simply doesn't exist as a valid application.

43:49.060 --> 43:50.840
But what it doesn't have is just as important.

43:51.000 --> 43:51.620
Expand on that.

43:51.980 --> 43:53.780
Why is omission so important here?

43:54.120 --> 43:59.620
It declares by omission that a passport application does not include your blood type or your favorite

43:59.620 --> 44:01.720
color or your high school GPA.

44:02.160 --> 44:06.420
It bounds the entire space of things an application can possibly be.

44:06.420 --> 44:08.420
It restricts the universe.

44:08.980 --> 44:12.760
An electrical outlet does the exact same thing through physical geometry.

44:13.300 --> 44:13.960
Oh, interesting.

44:14.300 --> 44:17.080
Think about a standard 120-volt wall socket.

44:18.060 --> 44:24.400
It demands two parallel prongs spaced exactly half an inch apart, A and D, a round grounding

44:24.400 --> 44:25.240
pin below them.

44:25.260 --> 44:25.460
Right.

44:25.660 --> 44:26.360
All at once.

44:26.620 --> 44:31.680
It accepts a plug of that specific shape and physically rejects anything shaped differently.

44:31.780 --> 44:33.060
You don't need a procedural check.

44:33.060 --> 44:36.640
You know, you don't need a tiny computer in the wall asking, are you a plug?

44:36.940 --> 44:38.100
No, the wall doesn't ask.

44:38.300 --> 44:42.880
The physical geometry has already ruled out inadmissible configurations before a single

44:42.880 --> 44:44.740
electron of current even flows.

44:45.080 --> 44:47.320
So a struct requires all of its field at once.

44:47.460 --> 44:48.700
It's an A and D space.

44:48.860 --> 44:49.160
Correct.

44:49.480 --> 44:54.160
But what if we have a situation in reality where it's not all of the above, but one or

44:54.160 --> 44:54.500
the other?

44:54.700 --> 44:55.820
This is where enums come in.

44:55.820 --> 45:01.820
The author describes enums as some state spaces, representing mutually exclusive possibilities.

45:02.340 --> 45:07.760
This is a critical transition in modeling reality because much of the real world is

45:07.760 --> 45:09.020
inherently mutually exclusive.

45:09.540 --> 45:10.820
It's an or-or relationship.

45:11.340 --> 45:12.060
Give me an example.

45:12.320 --> 45:14.100
Think of a traffic light at an intersection.

45:14.660 --> 45:18.040
It is red, or-or it is yellow, or-or it is green.

45:18.560 --> 45:20.140
It is never a blend of the three.

45:20.300 --> 45:21.600
Don't get a reddish green light.

45:21.740 --> 45:21.980
Never.

45:21.980 --> 45:26.440
And it is never a completely fourth unannounced color, like purple.

45:26.620 --> 45:28.480
If it were purple, you'd have total chaos.

45:28.700 --> 45:29.500
People would crash.

45:29.620 --> 45:32.740
Drivers wouldn't know whether to stop, go, or do a U-turn.

45:33.840 --> 45:38.280
The rules of the intersection rely on the state space being totally closed.

45:38.560 --> 45:39.000
Closed.

45:39.220 --> 45:44.760
The set of possible states is small, fixed, and known in advance to absolutely everyone

45:44.760 --> 45:45.880
interacting with the system.

45:46.540 --> 45:48.840
A jury verdict is another perfect sum space.

45:48.840 --> 45:52.160
The verdict is guilty, or not guilty.

45:52.240 --> 45:54.880
There is no third blank line on the form for maybe.

45:55.740 --> 45:56.760
Or kind of guilty.

45:56.980 --> 45:57.120
Right.

45:57.500 --> 46:02.960
The legal process forces the massive ambiguity and chaos of human deliberation to resolve

46:02.960 --> 46:05.000
into a tightly closed list of possibilities.

46:05.540 --> 46:08.660
That's the only way the legal machinery can proceed to the sentencing phase.

46:08.780 --> 46:09.560
A chess piece.

46:09.560 --> 46:16.760
A chess piece is a knight, or a pawn, or a rook, or a bishop, or a queen, or a king.

46:17.120 --> 46:22.440
You can't just invent a seventh piece mid-game, like, I don't know, a wizard, and put it on

46:22.440 --> 46:22.840
the board.

46:22.980 --> 46:24.860
The rules of chess wouldn't know what to do with a wizard.

46:24.980 --> 46:25.420
Exactly.

46:25.600 --> 46:26.600
The system would break.

46:27.460 --> 46:32.020
But the author notes that in a lot of traditional software design, we actually do try to invent

46:32.020 --> 46:33.460
a seventh piece all the time.

46:33.500 --> 46:33.860
We do.

46:34.020 --> 46:35.760
And it's a massive structural flaw.

46:35.760 --> 46:41.200
In languages without strict enums, programmers often try to simulate these mutually exclusive

46:41.200 --> 46:45.640
states using arbitrary integers, what we call magic numbers, or raw text strings.

46:45.840 --> 46:45.920
Right.

46:46.060 --> 46:48.620
They say, if the status is 1, it's green.

46:49.160 --> 46:50.320
If it's 2, it's red.

46:50.600 --> 46:52.040
But the compiler just sees a number?

46:52.240 --> 46:52.940
Just an integer.

46:53.220 --> 46:54.780
It doesn't know 3 is invalid.

46:55.100 --> 47:00.360
The possibilities only live in a comment somewhere in the code or in the programmer's head, not

47:00.360 --> 47:02.540
in the actual rigid structure of the system.

47:02.700 --> 47:04.360
And that is a recipe for disaster.

47:04.360 --> 47:11.020
If the possibilities are left implicit, every single piece of code that inspects the value

47:11.020 --> 47:15.540
has to independently reconstruct the list of what's allowed and manually check for invalid

47:15.540 --> 47:15.960
numbers.

47:16.080 --> 47:17.140
Which they will forget to do.

47:17.360 --> 47:17.720
Always.

47:18.900 --> 47:26.200
By using a true enum, the list of possibilities is closed, disjoint, and declared exactly once.

47:27.120 --> 47:31.040
The state space is mathematically just the sum of its variance.

47:31.040 --> 47:36.060
And when you have an enum, when you have this sum space, you have to extract the information

47:36.060 --> 47:36.580
safely.

47:36.780 --> 47:39.600
You have to figure out which reality you are currently in.

47:39.860 --> 47:40.080
Yes.

47:40.260 --> 47:43.540
This brings us to pattern matching as reconstruction.

47:44.040 --> 47:44.380
Yes.

47:44.560 --> 47:46.940
And the word reconstruction here is absolutely vital.

47:47.380 --> 47:51.640
When you use pattern matching in Rust, you are not just checking a label that was passively

47:51.640 --> 47:57.140
sitting on the value, you are reconstructing reality from a fixed list of candidates.

47:57.300 --> 47:58.960
Like a doctor diagnosing a patient.

47:59.140 --> 47:59.540
Exactly.

47:59.760 --> 48:03.140
A doctor doesn't just read a barcode on your forehead that says strep throat.

48:03.380 --> 48:04.080
I wish they could.

48:04.220 --> 48:04.440
Right.

48:04.560 --> 48:09.380
They take the symptoms, the blood test results, the fever, and they logically reconstruct reality

48:09.380 --> 48:11.180
from a fixed list of known diseases.

48:11.520 --> 48:13.600
They go down the enum of human illness.

48:13.680 --> 48:14.700
They reconstruct the state.

48:14.700 --> 48:17.680
And a good doctor doesn't stop after the first plausible guess.

48:17.840 --> 48:19.620
They have to exhaust the possibilities.

48:20.440 --> 48:22.720
Or an auditor reconciling a financial ledger.

48:23.320 --> 48:29.120
Every single mysterious transaction has to eventually fit exactly into a known, predefined

48:29.120 --> 48:30.380
tax category.

48:30.920 --> 48:32.820
You can't leave it as mystery money.

48:33.700 --> 48:36.500
This brings us to a major pitfall the author highlights.

48:37.000 --> 48:39.940
And it's something that catches almost everyone off guard.

48:40.560 --> 48:43.580
The danger of the catch-all arm in pattern matching.

48:43.580 --> 48:44.740
Oh, this is so good.

48:44.800 --> 48:46.140
Let's really explain this.

48:46.280 --> 48:46.380
Okay.

48:46.440 --> 48:50.620
So when you pattern match on an enum, the Rust compiler strictly requires you to handle

48:50.620 --> 48:52.240
every single possible variant.

48:52.440 --> 48:54.000
It enforces exhaustiveness.

48:54.300 --> 48:54.480
Okay.

48:54.860 --> 48:58.160
If the traffic light has three colors, your code must have three branches.

48:58.480 --> 48:58.780
Period.

48:59.200 --> 49:01.140
But the language does offer an escape hatch.

49:01.300 --> 49:01.500
Yeah.

49:01.780 --> 49:02.500
A catch-all arm.

49:02.880 --> 49:03.440
An underscore.

49:03.740 --> 49:03.900
Right.

49:03.920 --> 49:07.360
Basically saying, for everything else I haven't explicitly named, do this.

49:07.500 --> 49:07.760
Right.

49:08.060 --> 49:11.940
If you use a catch-all, you are willfully opting out of that exhaustiveness guarantee.

49:11.940 --> 49:16.080
So if I use a wildcard catch-all, it's like that doctor looking at my symptoms and saying,

49:16.260 --> 49:19.800
well, it's not a broken arm and it's not a heart attack, so for everything else it's

49:19.800 --> 49:20.660
probably just a cold.

49:20.820 --> 49:21.060
Yeah.

49:21.160 --> 49:22.660
Without running any more tests.

49:22.880 --> 49:23.080
Yeah.

49:23.260 --> 49:26.800
Just throwing every other possible disease in the universe into the cold bucket.

49:27.080 --> 49:27.520
Exactly.

49:27.860 --> 49:31.760
It feels efficient in the moment to write code that way.

49:31.880 --> 49:32.700
It saves typing.

49:32.920 --> 49:34.400
It clears the compiler error.

49:35.360 --> 49:38.280
But it is fundamentally dishonest to the system.

49:38.540 --> 49:39.400
Because it's lying.

49:39.400 --> 49:41.740
Because think about what happens a year later.

49:42.540 --> 49:44.520
What if a new variant is added to the enum?

49:45.040 --> 49:47.500
What if a new color is added to the traffic light?

49:47.680 --> 49:50.520
Or a new error state is added to the network protocol?

49:50.800 --> 49:52.940
The catch-all arm will silently swallow it.

49:53.040 --> 49:54.120
It won't warn you.

49:54.280 --> 50:00.080
It will treat the brand new catastrophic network failure exactly like a routine timeout.

50:00.220 --> 50:00.520
Yes.

50:00.720 --> 50:02.480
You aren't reconstructing reality.

50:02.940 --> 50:04.300
You are deferring the question.

50:04.300 --> 50:10.220
You are blindly absorbing future changes without verifying if your catch-all logic actually

50:10.220 --> 50:10.940
applies to them.

50:11.000 --> 50:13.560
It's like putting a bucket under a small leak in the ceiling.

50:13.800 --> 50:19.660
And then when the entire roof caves in during a hurricane, the bucket just silently overflows.

50:20.000 --> 50:20.960
It doesn't help at all.

50:21.120 --> 50:23.740
You told the system you had it handled, but you really didn't.

50:23.740 --> 50:26.760
And the author points out another way we bypass these guarantees.

50:27.480 --> 50:29.900
A single public field in a struct.

50:30.100 --> 50:30.400
Right.

50:30.580 --> 50:33.120
We talked about how structs bound reality.

50:33.920 --> 50:38.500
If you carefully design a constructor, a function that creates the struct, to ensure

50:38.500 --> 50:44.960
that, say, a latitude value is mathematically always between negative 90 and positive 90 degrees,

50:44.960 --> 50:49.000
you've narrowed the effective state space to something geographically safe.

50:49.660 --> 50:51.540
The rest of the program can totally trust it.

50:52.000 --> 50:54.880
But if you make that internal latitude field public...

50:54.880 --> 50:59.240
Any other part of the program can just bypass your careful constructor and directly write

50:59.240 --> 51:00.540
91 degrees into it.

51:00.660 --> 51:01.800
You shattered the guarantee.

51:02.340 --> 51:06.420
You put a highly advanced biometric lock on the front door, but you left the ground floor

51:06.420 --> 51:09.940
window wide open with a huge neon sign pointing to it.

51:09.940 --> 51:15.420
A single unguarded path back into the type restores the full, unconstrained, dangerous

51:15.420 --> 51:17.300
shape the fields originally declared.

51:17.560 --> 51:18.040
Precisely.

51:18.420 --> 51:22.060
You lose the ability to reason locally, because now you have to check every part of the code

51:22.060 --> 51:25.600
base to see if anyone sneakily changed the latitude through the open window.

51:25.860 --> 51:28.440
Okay, so we've defined the exact shape of our data.

51:28.700 --> 51:29.700
We know what it is.

51:29.860 --> 51:31.900
We have our structs and our enums locked down.

51:32.740 --> 51:37.900
But defining what a thing is only gets us so far in building a dynamic system.

51:38.020 --> 51:38.280
Right.

51:38.440 --> 51:39.440
Things have to do stuff.

51:39.440 --> 51:42.140
We also need to define what a thing can do.

51:42.500 --> 51:45.480
And we need to do that without caring about its internal makeup.

51:46.100 --> 51:52.100
This is where we move from representation to behavior, traits, generics, and composition.

51:52.540 --> 51:54.320
This is a really beautiful part of the theory.

51:54.480 --> 51:56.600
We move from asking, what are you made of?

51:56.680 --> 51:56.960
Yeah.

51:57.120 --> 52:00.080
To asking, what commitments can you honor?

52:00.360 --> 52:00.680
Okay.

52:00.880 --> 52:03.040
Traits in Rust are interface contracts.

52:03.280 --> 52:06.420
They define behavior absolutely independent of representation.

52:06.420 --> 52:07.740
The shipping contract.

52:07.740 --> 52:09.520
This is such a good analogy from the text.

52:09.840 --> 52:11.440
Think about a commercial shipping contract.

52:11.580 --> 52:11.720
Okay.

52:11.960 --> 52:15.880
It guarantees delivery time, weight limits, insurance liability, and price.

52:16.100 --> 52:18.440
But it does not guarantee which truck is used.

52:18.580 --> 52:18.700
Right.

52:18.860 --> 52:22.800
It doesn't guarantee the route the driver takes, what color the truck is, or which specific

52:22.800 --> 52:25.500
warehouse the package sits in at 2 a.m. on a Tuesday.

52:25.500 --> 52:29.520
I, as the retailer shipping a product, do not care about the truck.

52:29.860 --> 52:31.320
I only care about the contract.

52:31.780 --> 52:32.140
Exactly.

52:32.760 --> 52:38.160
The interface contract specifies observable commitments, not internal implementations.

52:38.540 --> 52:40.800
Think of an electrical appliance and a wall outlet.

52:40.880 --> 52:41.140
Okay.

52:41.320 --> 52:42.780
They share a strict contract.

52:43.240 --> 52:48.220
The outlet promises a certain voltage and frequency through a specific physical corrector.

52:48.780 --> 52:52.580
The appliance promises not to draw more than 15 amps.

52:52.580 --> 52:55.960
The outlet doesn't promise which power plant generated the electricity.

52:56.500 --> 52:58.300
It could be wind, solar, or coal.

52:59.040 --> 52:59.960
The appliance doesn't care.

53:00.560 --> 53:05.900
A USB port connects to a mechanical keyboard, a massive external storage drive, or a tiny

53:05.900 --> 53:07.260
webcam indiscriminately.

53:07.340 --> 53:10.800
It just says, speak this specific digital protocol and we're good.

53:10.880 --> 53:12.060
I don't care what you actually are.

53:12.200 --> 53:12.540
Exactly.

53:12.640 --> 53:16.560
And when we write code that depends on these traits instead of concrete types, we get generics.

53:17.000 --> 53:21.440
The author calls this parametric admissibility, deferring the choice of type without

53:21.440 --> 53:22.440
deferring the guarantee.

53:22.860 --> 53:25.960
Parametric admissibility is a very powerful concept in logic.

53:26.700 --> 53:30.020
A mathematician can prove a complex theorem about injective functions.

53:30.340 --> 53:30.560
Right.

53:30.720 --> 53:35.240
The proof doesn't need to know if the function is operating on real numbers, or complex matrices,

53:35.560 --> 53:37.420
or geometric shapes, or even sandwiches.

53:37.860 --> 53:38.680
Sandwiches, right.

53:38.780 --> 53:43.620
The logical proof holds for absolutely anything that satisfies the stated property of being

53:43.620 --> 53:44.160
injective.

53:44.160 --> 53:46.000
A standardized shipping container.

53:46.000 --> 53:53.220
A steel shipping container holds luxury furniture, or greasy machine parts, or refrigerated bananas

53:53.220 --> 53:54.020
equally well.

53:54.140 --> 53:54.240
Right.

53:54.880 --> 53:59.340
The container's structural design is constrained by external dimensions and weight limits,

53:59.340 --> 54:02.000
not by a specific cargo.

54:02.000 --> 54:07.160
It defers the choice of cargo until the very last second, but the guarantee that it will

54:07.160 --> 54:11.180
fit perfectly on the cargo ship's deck and lock into the crane is absolute.

54:11.180 --> 54:17.460
And this leads to a fundamental decades-old debate in system design and object-oriented programming.

54:18.080 --> 54:19.360
Composition versus inheritance.

54:19.560 --> 54:19.840
Yes.

54:20.380 --> 54:21.520
The base blender problem.

54:21.780 --> 54:25.540
Let's really unpack this, because anyone who has worked in a big corporate software system,

54:25.540 --> 54:29.020
or honestly just worked in a big bureaucracy, will feel this in their bones.

54:29.200 --> 54:29.620
They will.

54:29.740 --> 54:32.040
Let's say a company designs kitchen appliances.

54:32.520 --> 54:36.000
They create a base blender blueprint, and it has 30 different methods.

54:36.340 --> 54:36.500
Yeah.

54:36.520 --> 54:40.440
Blend, puree, crush ice, knead dough, heat soup, cool drinks.

54:40.500 --> 54:41.220
A super blender.

54:41.460 --> 54:45.580
And the rule is, every new appliance they make inherits from this base blender.

54:46.000 --> 54:48.580
Which works fine if you are making a slightly bigger blender.

54:49.080 --> 54:54.720
But what happens when the company invents a simple, battery-powered, handheld milk frother?

54:54.720 --> 54:55.200
Right.

54:55.520 --> 55:01.240
If the milk frother inherits from the base blender blueprint, it suddenly has to answer to the command,

55:01.600 --> 55:02.700
knead dough.

55:03.080 --> 55:03.600
Which is...

55:03.600 --> 55:04.260
Which is absurd.

55:04.380 --> 55:07.220
It's a tiny wire whisk running on AA batteries.

55:07.640 --> 55:11.880
It either has to write a bunch of fake boilerplate code to just ignore the dough command,

55:12.120 --> 55:16.020
or it physically snaps in half and crashes when a user tries to run it.

55:16.040 --> 55:21.680
Or consider a biological taxonomy system in a zoo's database, where a flightless bird class

55:21.680 --> 55:23.600
inherits from a base bird class.

55:23.600 --> 55:29.100
If the base bird class has a fly method, an ostrich is suddenly burdened with a logical

55:29.100 --> 55:31.520
obligation it physically cannot honor.

55:31.680 --> 55:34.560
It's forced to carry baggage it doesn't need and can't use.

55:34.820 --> 55:35.960
Let me try to summarize this.

55:36.100 --> 55:36.400
Go ahead.

55:36.700 --> 55:40.320
Inheritance is like going to a hardware store because you need to tighten one single screw.

55:40.700 --> 55:45.720
But instead of letting you buy a screwdriver, the store forces you to buy an entire 100-piece

55:45.720 --> 55:46.820
mechanics tool set.

55:47.080 --> 55:47.260
Right.

55:47.260 --> 55:50.840
And now you have to lug around a chainsaw you don't know how to use, and a blowtorch

55:50.840 --> 55:53.500
you don't need just because they came in the hardware base class box.

55:53.600 --> 55:54.000
Exactly.

55:54.260 --> 55:56.400
Composition is just picking up the screwdriver.

55:56.720 --> 55:58.360
That is the exact distinction.

55:58.960 --> 56:02.500
Single inheritance bundles obligations indivisibly.

56:03.160 --> 56:07.960
It forces subtypes to accept capabilities they can't actually implement honestly.

56:07.960 --> 56:10.740
It creates bloat and logical contradiction.

56:10.940 --> 56:11.700
That's messy.

56:12.340 --> 56:16.180
Composition, using traits, preserves distinguishability.

56:16.620 --> 56:19.060
You select exactly the obligations you can honor.

56:19.560 --> 56:23.920
A type's capability set should be exactly what its author can honestly implement.

56:24.280 --> 56:25.160
No more, no less.

56:25.540 --> 56:28.400
If you are a milk frother, you implement the froth trait.

56:28.820 --> 56:30.560
You do not implement the neato trait.

56:30.560 --> 56:33.140
And the manuscript points out the flip side of this, too.

56:33.800 --> 56:39.280
If you use overbroad generic bounds, meaning if you demand that a tool must be able to

56:39.280 --> 56:45.280
do X, Y, and Z when your specific job only actually requires X, you are needlessly shrinking

56:45.280 --> 56:46.620
the pool of valid tools.

56:46.840 --> 56:47.120
Yes.

56:47.260 --> 56:51.100
You are turning away the perfectly good milk frother because it can't crush ice, even though

56:51.100 --> 56:52.700
your recipe only asked to froth milk.

56:52.800 --> 56:52.980
Right.

56:53.160 --> 56:56.260
It's a total failure of locality at the behavioral level.

56:56.260 --> 57:02.300
You are demanding more context and more capability than the system actually requires to be correct.

57:02.620 --> 57:07.660
You're rejecting perfectly valid state transitions for no justifiable reason.

57:08.360 --> 57:15.000
It's truly fascinating how all these rules, aliasing, borrowing, enums, traits, are all

57:15.000 --> 57:18.440
just different mechanisms trying to keep the system strictly honest.

57:18.600 --> 57:19.400
Honesty is the goal.

57:19.740 --> 57:24.340
Which brings us to the final hurdle, the grand finale of the author's argument.

57:24.340 --> 57:30.620
We've built these perfectly defined, tightly constrained, beautifully composed logical systems.

57:31.020 --> 57:31.140
Right.

57:31.460 --> 57:37.820
But eventually, inevitably, they have to interact with the messy, unpredictable, analog, real

57:37.820 --> 57:38.240
world.

57:38.620 --> 57:39.620
What happens when they fail?

57:39.820 --> 57:42.620
This is where we confront partial functions and the edge of definition.

57:43.120 --> 57:47.440
There is often a dangerous gap between a claimed domain, what a function's signature says

57:47.440 --> 57:52.160
it enthusiastically accepts, and an honest domain, what actually works in reality when the rubber

57:52.160 --> 57:52.840
meets the road.

57:52.840 --> 57:54.420
The vending machine coin slot.

57:54.480 --> 57:55.460
Let's visualize this.

57:55.820 --> 57:57.060
You walk up to a vending machine.

57:57.800 --> 58:00.660
The slot physically accepts anything roughly coin-shaped.

58:01.340 --> 58:07.060
A quarter, a nickel, a perfectly flat steel washer, a foreign coin, a plastic token.

58:07.720 --> 58:09.020
That is the claimed domain.

58:09.340 --> 58:11.320
The geometry of the slot makes a claim.

58:11.720 --> 58:12.900
I accept disks.

58:13.260 --> 58:14.540
But internally, what happens?

58:14.620 --> 58:17.240
Internally, the machine's honest domain is much stricter.

58:17.240 --> 58:22.040
It relies on magnets, weight sensors, and lasers to test the metallic composition and

58:22.040 --> 58:22.400
size.

58:22.580 --> 58:25.040
It only accepts real, local currency.

58:25.340 --> 58:25.460
Right.

58:25.660 --> 58:30.320
So it swallows the washer, spins it through the mechanism, realizes it's fake, and spits

58:30.320 --> 58:31.420
it into the coin return.

58:31.820 --> 58:33.020
It rejects it downstream.

58:33.540 --> 58:36.200
Or consider a recipe calling for one lemon.

58:36.480 --> 58:36.760
Okay.

58:37.340 --> 58:40.180
The claimed domain is simply any lemon you can buy at the store.

58:40.180 --> 58:43.900
But the honest domain is a lemon that actually has juice inside it.

58:44.120 --> 58:44.600
Oh, yeah.

58:44.760 --> 58:47.800
If you use a lemon that's been sitting on your kitchen counter for six months and has

58:47.800 --> 58:51.560
dried into a solid, hollow rock, it fits the claimed domain.

58:52.120 --> 58:55.200
It is technically a lemon, but it utterly fails the honest domain.

58:55.340 --> 58:56.700
Your recipe will fail.

58:56.960 --> 59:03.940
So how do we handle this gap between claim and reality in software or in any logical system?

59:04.180 --> 59:07.120
How do we handle absence or failure honestly?

59:07.120 --> 59:11.240
The author says we have to formally name the hole in the state space.

59:11.400 --> 59:11.560
Yes.

59:11.660 --> 59:13.060
This is the concept of option.

59:13.600 --> 59:15.940
Option is a topological view of absence.

59:16.200 --> 59:20.520
And what I mean by topology here is the actual mathematical shape of the possibilities.

59:20.840 --> 59:20.980
Okay.

59:21.200 --> 59:22.740
Imagine a massive parking lot.

59:23.060 --> 59:24.620
Space number 57 might be empty.

59:24.940 --> 59:30.760
If your digital tracking system doesn't have a distinct, formalized, vacant state, what do

59:30.760 --> 59:31.120
you do?

59:31.280 --> 59:32.260
You invent one.

59:32.660 --> 59:36.340
You might put in a dummy license plate like zero, zero, zero, zero, zero.

59:36.340 --> 59:36.600
Right.

59:36.700 --> 59:38.140
You assume a ghost car is there.

59:38.580 --> 59:42.200
A census form for an unmarried person needs a real blank state.

59:42.700 --> 59:47.040
If the government database doesn't have a concept of none, you might force the clerk to write

59:47.040 --> 59:49.060
N.A. as the spouse's name.

59:49.240 --> 59:49.820
And now?

59:49.940 --> 59:54.580
And now, somewhere in a mainframe, your database literally thinks there is a living citizen married

59:54.580 --> 59:56.080
to a person named N.A.

59:56.240 --> 01:00:01.120
Which will eventually cause a cascading failure when this system tries to mail a tax form to

01:00:01.120 --> 01:00:02.220
Mr. and Mrs. N.A.

01:00:02.220 --> 01:00:09.060
If a language uses nulls, which most traditional languages do, it's pretending the hole in the

01:00:09.060 --> 01:00:11.500
state space is just a regular valid point.

01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:15.500
It conflates the absence of a thing with the thing itself.

01:00:15.720 --> 01:00:15.840
Right.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:21.300
Option, on the other hand, is a disjoint sum of the expected type and a single distinguished

01:00:21.300 --> 01:00:23.380
whole, which Rust calls none.

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:29.020
It structurally forces you to explicitly handle the absence before you can touch the data.

01:00:29.020 --> 01:00:33.260
So when a programmer uses a null value, they are essentially putting a plastic clothing

01:00:33.260 --> 01:00:34.600
mannequin in a theater seat.

01:00:34.800 --> 01:00:36.160
That's exactly what they're doing.

01:00:36.260 --> 01:00:38.800
It looks like a person to the ticketing system from a distance.

01:00:39.040 --> 01:00:44.120
It physically fits in the seat, but the usher's logic crashes completely when they try to hand

01:00:44.120 --> 01:00:45.900
it a playbill or ask for its ticket.

01:00:46.100 --> 01:00:46.340
Right.

01:00:46.620 --> 01:00:50.560
By using option, we are forcing the system to be honest about the gap in reality.

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:54.840
We are forcing the usher to acknowledge that the seat is empty before they try to talk to it.

